{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 42, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "\"the social network\" is a thought-provoking film that delves into the fascinating story of facebook's creation and the tumultuous journey of its founder, mark zuckerberg. directed by david fincher, the movie is a riveting portrayal of the complexities of ambition, friendship, and the consequences of success.\n\nthe film's narrative is presented through a series of flashbacks, as various characters recount their experiences with zuckerberg and the early days of facebook. this non-linear storytelling approach adds depth and complexity to the plot, allowing the audience to piece together the events that shaped the social media giant.\n\njesse eisenberg delivers a captivating performance as mark zuckerberg, capturing the character's intensity, awkwardness, and driven personality. his portrayal is both nuanced and relatable, making it easy to understand the motivations behind zuckerberg's actions. andrew garfield and justin timberlake also shine in their respective roles as eduardo saverin and sean parker, bringing depth and charisma to their characters.\n\none of the standout aspects of \"the social network\" is its exploration of the themes that underpin the story. the film raises important questions about the nature of friendship, the consequences of ambition, and the impact of technology on society. it also touches on issues of class, privilege, and the cutthroat world of entrepreneurship.\n\nthe movie's cinematography is sleek and modern, with a predominantly dark and muted color palette that reflects the film's tone. the score, composed by trent reznor and atticus ross, is equally impressive, featuring a haunting and atmospheric soundtrack that perfectly complements the on-screen action.\n\noverall, \"the social network\" is a gripping and thought-provoking film that offers a fascinating glimpse into the world of facebook and its creat